      "anchor": "mapping#markdown-3",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "label": "4. How it maps to the protocol",
      "excerpt": "Reading is the protocol's job. Each org ATSpace uses the member-list policy, so",
      "text": "Convert to a table like before.  1st 2 bullets into one row with `Reading is the protocol's job` ad the left column",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-02T01:31:34.134Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Each org ATSpace uses the member-list policy, so the host refuses anyone not on the list.\nA public ATSpace's list holds everyone in the org, and a private ATSpace's holds its Leads and those above them.\nSpell never gates a read. If a private thing is shown to the wrong person, whoever decided is liable, and checking on every file access would give Spell that exposure every time.\nSpell keeps the lists right instead: it works out each list from the org's roles, and updates it when someone joins, leaves, changes role or reaches the end of a term. A credential already issued runs out within about 10 minutes.\nThat can be audited: Spell can compare each ATSpace's list with what the roles say, and report any difference. It's a rare, checkable job, not a decision on every read.\nSpell adds no read rules inside an ATSpace: anyone on a list can read all of it with any AT Protocol app, so a rule only Spell's apps obey protects nothing.\nSo which ATSpace a thing is in decides who can see it. What someone mustn't see goes in an ATSpace they aren't on.\nSpell apps read through a small proxy, because a page likely can't read an ATSpace directly. It passes each person's own credential to the host, and the host decides. It holds no data, and keeps no shared copy, as answering people from one would mean Spell deciding who may read.\nA local cache is fine: a person's own browser or phone can keep what they've already read, for speed and for offline. They were allowed to read it, and it's theirs alone. When the host next refuses them, the app clears that ATSpace's cache.",
      "offset": 31,
      "edited": "2026-11-02T01:32:00.088Z",
